00:00:13,576 --> 00:00:18,015
an AI agent, but you should be able to
trust the system that you build around it.

6
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:23,126
So my toolkit is AWS because I work for
AWS, but the concepts and patterns that

7
00:00:23,126 --> 00:00:27,456
I'm gonna talk to you about throughout
this presentation can apply to any

8
00:00:27,456 --> 00:00:29,086
agent that you're building anywhere.

9
00:00:29,756 --> 00:00:33,735
So first I wanna talk about how
AI agents break in creative ways.

10
00:00:33,826 --> 00:00:38,546
Because models are naive, sometimes
they approach problems in ways that

11
00:00:38,555 --> 00:00:40,275
we humans would never consider.

12
00:00:40,565 --> 00:00:44,216
And so sometimes agents break in ways
where they didn't mean to, but they did

13
00:00:44,216 --> 00:00:46,125
something that was maybe off policy.

14
00:00:46,456 --> 00:00:50,335
And other times agents do the wrong
thing because there are adversarial

15
00:00:50,335 --> 00:00:54,446
users that are trying to manipulate
them into behaving a certain way.

16
00:00:54,736 --> 00:00:58,945
So here's some examples of how AI agents
