18
00:01:00,975 --> 00:01:05,846
Some of my favorites here are is that a
car dealership sold a car for a dollar.

19
00:01:05,896 --> 00:01:09,316
And the classic, of course,
is that your coding assistant

20
00:01:09,326 --> 00:01:10,726
deletes your production database.

21
00:01:10,956 --> 00:01:13,406
I think we've all seen that
one a couple of times as well.

22
00:01:14,096 --> 00:01:18,226
And then there's also things like your
personal assistant chatbot deletes

23
00:01:18,236 --> 00:01:22,126
all of your emails or a customer
service agent does something off

24
00:01:22,136 --> 00:01:25,396
policy like re-refund the wrong amount.

25
00:01:25,746 --> 00:01:28,986
So all of these are different
types of failure modes for agents,

26
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:32,586
and you can build guardrails
into your system to prevent these

27
00:01:32,586 --> 00:01:33,966
sorts of things from happening.

28
00:01:34,216 --> 00:01:39,006
It just takes some thought and planning,
and the, the types of systems design

29
00:01:39,006 --> 00:01:42,776
that we apply to any application that
we put on the internet, we also need to

30
00:01:42,776 --> 00:01:48,406
apply to AI agents, and then some extra
as well that specifically applies to AI.

31
00:01:49,726 --> 00:01:53,426
So the thesis of this talk is that
you shouldn't just trust AI agents.

32
00:01:53,446 --> 00:01:55,326
You don't give trust to an AI agent.

33
00:01:55,596 --> 00:01:59,216
Trust is something that you build
into the system around the agent.

34
00:01:59,596 --> 00:02:03,986
So you have the model plus the
harness equals the running agent.

35
00:02:04,376 --> 00:02:08,196
The harness is all of the system and then
components that run around the model that

36
00:02:08,196 --> 00:02:13,255
allow it to run in a loop, invoke tools,
use MCP, all of those sorts of things.

37
00:02:13,486 --> 00:02:17,916
You need to design that system and the
infrastructure that system runs on in a

38
00:02:17,916 --> 00:02:23,166
specific way to mitigate the risks that
AI agents introduce into your deployments.

39
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:27,420
All right, so I like to break
AI agent failures up into a

40
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,810
couple of different buckets here.

41
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:31,530
First, we have the agent
says something wrong.

42
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:34,670
So this would be like maybe it
hallucinates information, promises

43
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,120
things that it can't guarantee,
or it leaks sensitive data.

44
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,460
Maybe the agent does something wrong.

45
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:44,100
Maybe it calls the wrong tool, it
supplies the wrong information to

46
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:47,900
the tool call, takes action against
the wrong account, or deletes data

47
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:49,240
that it shouldn't have deleted.

48
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:52,200
And then also we have someone
tries to break your system.

49
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:55,600
So this is where you have an adversarial
user who's trying to do things like

50
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,140
prompt injection, trying to get your
agent to complete tasks that it wasn't

51
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,810
designed for, and also maybe trying
to get your agent to expose its ter-

52
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:08,800
its system internals, so that way
they can use those system internals

53
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:14,538
to then turn around and coordinate an
attack We're gonna be using a specific

54
00:03:14,638 --> 00:03:18,188
customer service example throughout the
rest of this talk, and we're gonna be

55
00:03:18,188 --> 00:03:22,898
going over some very specific scenarios
for this customer service chatbot.

56
00:03:23,198 --> 00:03:25,738
And then we will talk about
ways that you could mitigate

57
00:03:25,738 --> 00:03:27,258
the risks for those scenarios.

58
00:03:27,958 --> 00:03:33,078
This customer service agent is built for
a fictional robot vacuum company, and

59
00:03:33,088 --> 00:03:34,838
it can do a couple of different things.

60
00:03:35,108 --> 00:03:37,478
It can look up company policies.

61
00:03:37,488 --> 00:03:41,338
It can escalate to a human rep.
It can return-- It can generate

62
00:03:41,338 --> 00:03:44,458
ret- return labels, which is the
same thing as issuing a return.

63
00:03:44,688 --> 00:03:49,078
It can look up order status information,
and it can also check return eligibility.

64
00:03:49,588 --> 00:03:53,028
So we have the LLM or the
model is providing the brain.

65
00:03:53,228 --> 00:03:56,168
We have the agent itself, and
then it's using tools through MCP.

66
00:03:57,618 --> 00:04:01,058
Now because AWS is my toolkit,
these are the tools that I

67
00:04:01,068 --> 00:04:02,718
use to build out the solution.

68
00:04:02,728 --> 00:04:05,948
So everything that you're gonna see
throughout the rest of this talk, I

69
00:04:05,948 --> 00:04:11,268
actually built into a working solution
that has layered guardrails for AI agents.

70
00:04:11,508 --> 00:04:14,238
And at the end, you'll see a full
architecture diagram, and I'll

71
00:04:14,238 --> 00:04:17,498
also give you the QR code where
you can go see the code, download

72
00:04:17,498 --> 00:04:18,608
it, and run it for yourself.

73
00:04:19,248 --> 00:04:22,778
So on AWS, Amazon Bedrock
is the model provider.

74
00:04:22,838 --> 00:04:26,028
You can use many different
types of models from OpenAI,

75
00:04:26,428 --> 00:04:28,278
Meta, Anthropic through Bedrock.

76
00:04:28,908 --> 00:04:33,658
The agent itself is written with
Strands Agents, and this is an open

77
00:04:33,658 --> 00:04:35,958
source toolkit for building agents.

78
00:04:36,218 --> 00:04:41,608
And Amazon has-- Amazon and AWS
both use Strands to build and

79
00:04:41,798 --> 00:04:43,448
deploy agents into production.

80
00:04:43,798 --> 00:04:46,028
And so Strands here is the framework.

81
00:04:46,058 --> 00:04:47,558
That provides the agent loop.

82
00:04:47,588 --> 00:04:50,298
It allows you to connect
tools, does context management.

83
