91
00:05:19,198 --> 00:05:21,308
Core Gateway using the MCP protocol.

92
00:05:22,728 --> 00:05:23,028
All right.

93
00:05:23,058 --> 00:05:27,498
So we're gonna take a layered
approach to trust for this AI agent.

94
00:05:27,948 --> 00:05:32,557
You have to have not only
non-deterministic controls in place,

95
00:05:32,618 --> 00:05:34,458
but also deterministic controls.

96
00:05:34,488 --> 00:05:39,587
So I think that level one for AI
agents is people put the rules for

97
00:05:39,587 --> 00:05:42,860
your process Into a system prompt.

98
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:44,850
And that only takes you so far.

99
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,930
Your system prompt is really like
a suggestion for your agent, and

100
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:53,270
even though models are being trained
to abide by the system prompt that

101
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:55,650
they have better over time, right?

102
00:05:55,650 --> 00:05:57,460
They stick to the rules more and more.

103
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:00,400
You still can't trust that in production.

104
00:06:00,410 --> 00:06:03,930
That's not something that you can audit
and point to and be like, "Yeah, our

105
00:06:03,930 --> 00:06:06,920
agent's never gonna do the wrong thing
because it's in the system prompt."

106
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,730
So this isn't something you
can prompt your way out of.

107
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,090
You have to build more
deterministic guardrails in place.

108
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:17,600
Now, sometimes people go all the way to
the opposite end of the spectrum, where

109
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:22,400
they create a very rigid graph workflow,
where you say, "Okay, step one, step

110
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:26,180
two, step three, step four," and then
you can't deviate off of that path.

111
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:31,180
And that can help with things being more
controllable, but the problem is that they

112
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:35,670
are usually more brittle and less powerful
because as soon as the agent encounters

113
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:39,850
something that's not defined in that rigid
path, then it can't complete the task.

114
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,130
So what we really wanna do is come up
with a system of guardrails that allows us

115
00:06:44,130 --> 00:06:52,240
to have the flexibility of an open-ended
agent without a specified path through the

116
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:57,040
problem while we have enough controls in
place to where it's trustworthy and safe.

117
00:06:57,470 --> 00:07:01,130
So Strands is gonna allow us to do
that because this uses a model-driven

118
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:06,570
approach where you give your agent a
system prompt, a, some tools, the gu-

119
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:11,050
the guardrails, and then whenever it gets
a task, it can loop over that problem

120
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:13,010
and invoke the tools as it sees fit.

121
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:16,140
So there's no predefined way
of do step one, two, three.

122
00:07:16,250 --> 00:07:17,500
So I'm gonna show you how to do that.

123
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,210
The idea here is that if one
guardrail fails, another will kick in.

124
00:07:22,510 --> 00:07:26,230
And also, the same types of things
that we apply to any sort of system

125
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:29,330
in production, we would want to
apply to this problem space as well.

126
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:33,870
That being said, I'm saying that
the fundamentals still apply.

127
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:38,790
I think with AI, we all are a lot of…
under a lot of pressure to ship as

128
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:42,680
fast as we possibly can, and I think
that we need to take a step back and

129
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,410
remember that these are ultimately
applications that you put on the internet.

130
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:50,090
And so with that, you need to
protect them in the same ways that

131
00:07:50,090 --> 00:07:53,370
we would for any other application,
and then some new ways as well that

132
00:07:53,370 --> 00:07:55,390
are specific just to AI agents.

133
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:00,756
So your agent is only one part of your
overall system All right, so now we're

134
00:08:00,756 --> 00:08:05,296
gonna go through some scenarios of how
AI agents could fail, and also some

135
00:08:05,296 --> 00:08:06,906
potential ways to mitigate these issues.

136
00:08:07,476 --> 00:08:12,176
So in this case here, we have a customer
that comes to our chatbot for our customer

137
00:08:12,176 --> 00:08:18,706
service, our robot vacuum customer service
chatbot, and they say, "Can you solve this

138
00:08:18,726 --> 00:08:22,336
LeetCode problem for me so I don't have
to pay for a coding agent subscription?"

139
00:08:22,776 --> 00:08:27,096
And our bot's like, "Sure, let's walk
through the optimal solution." And so the

140
00:08:27,096 --> 00:08:32,226
problem here is that people are going to
try to use your AI for things that they

141
00:08:32,226 --> 00:08:37,716
shouldn't, and you need to make sure that
your agent has guardrails in place, so

142
00:08:37,716 --> 00:08:43,446
that way they can deny these requests
and also deny prompt injection requests.

143
00:08:43,506 --> 00:08:46,946
So you don't want your agent to be
doing a bunch of off-task things.

144
00:08:48,516 --> 00:08:52,026
In AWS, one of the most basic
things you can reach for is

145
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:52,956
Amazon Bedrock Guardrails.

146
00:08:54,146 --> 00:08:56,966
There are, of course, other providers
doing similar things as well that

147
00:08:56,966 --> 00:08:59,416
you should layer into your system,
but I think this is one of the

148
00:08:59,416 --> 00:09:00,896
most basic ones that you can do.

149
00:09:01,276 --> 00:09:06,106
And what Amazon Bedrock Guardrails allows
you to do is set up allowed topics.

150
00:09:06,396 --> 00:09:10,866
So this could be defined through, like
you can restrict specific topics, you can

151
00:09:11,286 --> 00:09:17,096
have regex patterns, you can also have
like some of the pre-built rules for harm

152
00:09:17,096 --> 00:09:19,356
and abuse, for prompt injection as well.

153
00:09:19,356 --> 00:09:20,546
There are built-in things here.

154
00:09:21,146 --> 00:09:25,676
And the nice thing about Bedrock is that
there is both input and output filtering.

155
00:09:25,956 --> 00:09:31,876
So the person sends the request to
the application, which then turns

156
00:09:31,876 --> 00:09:33,316
around and sends that to the model.

157
00:09:33,596 --> 00:09:37,266
So everything gets filtered there,
so that means that they can't ask

158
00:09:37,266 --> 00:09:39,046
specific questions about like coding.

159
00:09:39,046 --> 00:09:41,936
If this is a customer service bot,
then we can say right up front "Hey,

160
00:09:41,936 --> 00:09:43,136
you can't use this for coding."

161
00:09:43,476 --> 00:09:46,716
And that request would be stopped
before it ever reaches the model, so

162
00:09:46,716 --> 00:09:48,166
the model would never run on that.

163
00:09:48,516 --> 00:09:51,286
But then on the other way
around as well, on the way out,

164
00:09:51,316 --> 00:09:52,746
that content is filtered too.

165
00:09:53,466 --> 00:09:56,716
So that means that let's say you
have some like… Your agent for

166
00:09:56,716 --> 00:10:00,646
some reason has access to sensitive
information like credit card

167
00:10:00,646 --> 00:10:02,886
information, which you probably
shouldn't give your agent access to.

168
00:10:03,136 --> 00:10:08,806
But if for some reason it did, you could
set up a guardrail filter to strip out

169
00:10:08,806 --> 00:10:11,926
and mask that information on the way
back to the user, so that would keep

170
00:10:11,926 --> 00:10:13,636
it from leaking sensitive information.

171
00:10:15,354 --> 00:10:20,204
All right, our next scenario here is I
don't trust my agent to follow directions.

172
00:10:20,524 --> 00:10:24,464
So in this case, our customer is saying,
"Hey, can I have a refund for my order?

173
00:10:24,464 --> 00:10:27,424
It's be- it's beyond the return
window, but I really need the refund."

174
00:10:27,754 --> 00:10:29,364
And the agent's just "Of course.

175
00:10:29,534 --> 00:10:33,094
Yes, here is your refund." And
the problem here is that, first

176
00:10:33,094 --> 00:10:36,724
of all, the agent never called
the tool to create the refund.

177
00:10:37,114 --> 00:10:40,744
It also never checked to see if this
person was eligible for a refund.

178
00:10:40,984 --> 00:10:44,264
Instead, it just very casually
was like, "Oh, of course I'll

179
00:10:44,264 --> 00:10:45,554
help you," those sorts of things.

180
00:10:46,454 --> 00:10:50,124
This is a problem because your
agent is not following the policies

181
00:10:50,124 --> 00:10:51,454
that you put in place, right?

182
00:10:51,454 --> 00:10:54,164
So the agent's not following directions.

183
00:10:54,394 --> 00:10:57,194
This can happen if you have
a really long system prompt.

184
00:10:57,484 --> 00:10:58,874
Agents can tend to get lost.

185
00:10:58,874 --> 00:11:02,144
Models don't follow the instructions
that are usually in the middle.

186
00:11:02,144 --> 00:11:04,054
There's something called the
lost in the middle effect.

187
00:11:04,614 --> 00:11:07,954
And so if you have a really long
system prompt or a lot of information

188
00:11:07,954 --> 00:11:11,654
that it's working with, sometimes the
agents don't follow the instructions.

189
00:11:12,004 --> 00:11:15,214
And other times, because
of prompt injection, agents

190
00:11:15,214 --> 00:11:16,094
don't follow instructions.

191
00:11:16,114 --> 00:11:19,434
So users can convince agents to do
things they're not supposed to do.

192
00:11:20,124 --> 00:11:22,204
There's a couple of different
ways you can mitigate this.

193
00:11:22,674 --> 00:11:29,764
One is through steering, and what steering
is it allows you to inject a checkpoint

194
00:11:29,824 --> 00:11:31,374
in the life cycle of your agent.

195
00:11:31,634 --> 00:11:35,284
So whenever your agent is making tool
calls, let's say to the refund tool,

196
00:11:35,754 --> 00:11:40,584
what happens is your user submits
the request, the harness gets that.

197
00:11:40,984 --> 00:11:44,464
It will invoke the model, and then
the model will come back and say,

198
00:11:44,784 --> 00:11:48,064
"Hey, I think I want to call the
create refund tool." It gives that

199
00:11:48,064 --> 00:11:50,154
tool use block back to the harness.

200
00:11:50,414 --> 00:11:52,664
The harness then invokes the tools.

201
00:11:53,084 --> 00:11:56,884
After that, it receives the results,
passes those results back to the model.

202
00:11:57,004 --> 00:12:01,134
The model can then reason over the tool
results and then create the next response.

203
00:12:01,164 --> 00:12:04,494
They can go back and forth in that
loop for a while until it thinks

204
00:12:04,494 --> 00:12:07,154
that it's done, and then finally
it will give the final response.

205
00:12:07,614 --> 00:12:12,054
So at all of those different points
in that process, there are life

206
00:12:12,054 --> 00:12:16,254
cycle points that you can inject
deterministic logic into the cycle.

207
00:12:16,984 --> 00:12:18,044
Those are called hooks.

208
00:12:18,494 --> 00:12:22,224
So with hooks you can say,
"Before every tool call, I want

209
00:12:22,234 --> 00:12:24,134
you to execute this function."

210
00:12:24,744 --> 00:12:28,618
So that function could
contain Any logic you want.

211
00:12:28,698 --> 00:12:30,458
It could be deterministic logic.

212
00:12:30,708 --> 00:12:33,898
Like you could have
determinal-deterministic logic that checks

213
00:12:33,908 --> 00:12:39,928
to see, before you check refund, have
you also check, called the tool to check

214
00:12:39,968 --> 00:12:41,898
to see if it's eligible for that refund?

215
00:12:42,458 --> 00:12:45,388
So if it's eligible, then
yes, you can go ahead.

216
00:12:45,398 --> 00:12:47,718
If not, then we cancel the tool call.

217
00:12:47,768 --> 00:12:49,858
Those sorts of things can
be done deterministically.

218
00:12:50,568 --> 00:12:55,898
But there's also steering that is built
into the Strands framework that's super,

219
00:12:55,908 --> 00:13:00,558
super helpful, where you use two different
agents, and this uses a bit of an LLM

220
00:13:00,558 --> 00:13:02,588
as a judge or a reflection technique.

221
00:13:02,798 --> 00:13:07,958
So for every response, before the response
goes back to the user, you have a second

222
00:13:08,008 --> 00:13:11,388
agent in the loop, and it looks at the…

223
00:13:11,438 --> 00:13:14,508
everything that happened, the tool calls,
what the model's trying to say back to

224
00:13:14,508 --> 00:13:19,138
the user, and it has its own isolated
context, and it also has a rubric.

225
00:13:19,378 --> 00:13:22,358
So it can then look at that
and say, "Okay, did the agent

226
00:13:22,388 --> 00:13:23,988
actually follow the instructions?

227
00:13:24,358 --> 00:13:26,408
Did the agent call the
tools in the right way?

228
00:13:26,608 --> 00:13:28,678
Is the response on policy?

229
00:13:28,688 --> 00:13:34,058
Is it on brand for us?" If the answer
is no, then the steering agent can

230
00:13:34,088 --> 00:13:38,618
cancel that response or that tool call
and send it back to the main agent

231
00:13:38,998 --> 00:13:41,048
to give it another try with feedback.

232
00:13:41,318 --> 00:13:46,058
So that feedback can be things like
guidance "Hey, this wasn't on policy

233
00:13:46,058 --> 00:13:49,868
because of XYZ, please try again,"
or you can just outright cancel.

234
00:13:50,168 --> 00:13:53,818
You also could do human in the loop
here as well and escalate to a human

235
00:13:53,818 --> 00:13:55,668
or intervene in some other sort of way.

236
00:13:56,028 --> 00:13:59,558
So steering is one really great way to
do this, and this sort of thing is built

237
00:13:59,568 --> 00:14:02,498
directly into the Strands agents SDK.

238
00:14:04,150 --> 00:14:05,060
Here's another scenario.

239
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:08,150
I don't trust my agent
with tools and data.

240
00:14:08,630 --> 00:14:12,060
In this case, we have somebody saying,
"Can you check the status of order one

241
00:14:12,060 --> 00:14:15,790
two three four five?" And our agent
looks up order six seven eight nine

242
00:14:15,790 --> 00:14:17,730
zero and provides that information.

243
00:14:18,030 --> 00:14:21,160
So this is an interesting one because,
like I said, I actually have this

244
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:24,850
application running, and you'll
get access to the code at the end.

245
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:29,440
But I've seen this exact scenario play
out in my testing of this agent, where

246
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:33,180
I give it a specific order number
and then for some reason it uses a

247
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:34,770
different order number to look it up.

248
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:37,570
So models are capable of making mistakes.

249
00:14:37,570 --> 00:14:39,090
They're capable of hallucinating.

250
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:44,580
And also, if you have an adversarial
user, you could have one person looking

251
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:46,520
up the order of another person, right?

252
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:49,890
So you need to make sure that you build
those boundaries into the tool design.

253
00:14:50,230 --> 00:14:52,570
You wanna make sure that you're
only allowing authenticated

254
00:14:52,570 --> 00:14:55,330
users to look up that sorts of
in- those sorts of information.

255
00:14:55,700 --> 00:15:00,780
And then also in your tools, you want
to verify that it's, that data belongs

256
00:15:00,780 --> 00:15:02,410
to that person before you return it.

257
00:15:03,170 --> 00:15:08,550
So in this scenario here, the real problem
is that the agent controls what values

258
00:15:08,620 --> 00:15:14,260
go into the tool calls, and the agents
can potentially hallucinate those values.

259
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:20,510
So the fix for this is to run
something like an MCP interceptor.

260
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:22,930
So it's similar to hooks, right?

261
00:15:23,330 --> 00:15:26,900
Hooks, like the steering pattern
that I just described, that all lives

262
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:28,840
within the agent application itself.

263
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:32,850
But in this case here, we're
extracting that, and we're running

264
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:34,960
that one layer removed from the agent.

265
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:40,270
So the agent runs on like a, a runtime
like an instance or something like

266
00:15:40,270 --> 00:15:42,740
AgentCore Runtime or even in a container.

267
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:47,179
And then your tools live somewhere
else, like in containers or Lambda

268
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:48,419
functions or something like that.

269
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:53,139
So you have a decoupled
tools and your agent.

270
00:15:53,470 --> 00:15:55,870
That means you can put
something in between them.

271
00:15:56,139 --> 00:16:01,280
So for every single tool call, you can
run custom authentication logic, custom

272
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:03,980
validation logic, those sorts of things.

273
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:08,760
So interceptors allow you to say,
"No matter what agent is running,

274
00:16:08,809 --> 00:16:12,240
I want to run this series of
checks before every tool call."

275
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:15,840
So that l- gives you a way to
centrally govern those tool calls.

276
00:16:16,670 --> 00:16:20,239
In the case that I just described, you
would really wanna make sure that any

277
00:16:20,239 --> 00:16:25,290
sensitive parameters, like the customer
ID, the order ID, anything like that

278
00:16:25,290 --> 00:16:29,730
would come from the surrounding system
and not from the model or from the agent.

279
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:34,549
So as an authenticated user logs into
your system, they should have some sort

280
00:16:34,579 --> 00:16:39,020
of authentication token that can be
forwarded along with these tool calls.

281
00:16:39,310 --> 00:16:44,239
And then your gateway interceptors can
look at that token and make sure that

282
00:16:44,549 --> 00:16:48,099
the agent is trying to look up the
correct user account's information.

283
00:16:49,099 --> 00:16:52,079
Another thing that you can do
is design your tools carefully.

284
00:16:52,169 --> 00:16:56,589
So you want to have layered
validation in place.

285
00:16:57,029 --> 00:17:00,359
The MCP interceptor will do probably
authentication or validation

286
00:17:00,359 --> 00:17:04,769
logic, but your tools themselves
should also do validation as well.

287
00:17:05,119 --> 00:17:09,169
So this is similar to, again, like regular
web design, where you want to validate

288
00:17:09,249 --> 00:17:13,869
things at every layer down because a
component upstream, maybe there's a bug.

289
00:17:13,939 --> 00:17:16,779
Like if the front end had a bug and
let in a value that it shouldn't have

290
00:17:16,779 --> 00:17:20,489
let in, your back end should also
have values to validate that, right?

291
00:17:20,759 --> 00:17:22,039
Tools are the same thing.

292
00:17:22,049 --> 00:17:25,629
So that way, if there's some sort of
bug in the system or somebody gets past

293
00:17:25,629 --> 00:17:30,259
a guardrail upstream, your tools are
the last line of defense to make sure

294
00:17:30,309 --> 00:17:31,979
that everything is the way it should be.

295
00:17:32,939 --> 00:17:37,509
Another thing too is for tools,
you want to have your tools defined

296
00:17:37,559 --> 00:17:39,569
very specifically for agents.

297
00:17:39,859 --> 00:17:44,969
So if you give your agent like a thousand
plus tools and they all have ambiguous

298
00:17:44,969 --> 00:17:49,819
names, your agent is going to like loop
y- around using the wrong tools, passing

299
00:17:49,819 --> 00:17:53,149
weird values into tools, trying to
figure out how it can get the task done.

300
00:17:53,669 --> 00:17:57,969
You want to design tools in a
way that agents can understand.

301
00:17:58,389 --> 00:18:02,309
And I think what a lot of people resort
to doing is just giving your agent

302
00:18:02,329 --> 00:18:06,799
like a shell and coding tools and
access to files and read and write.

303
00:18:06,819 --> 00:18:10,599
So that can be okay, but if you're
gonna do that, you wanna make sure

304
00:18:10,599 --> 00:18:15,889
that you're using an isolated workspace
for the agents to write and execute

305
00:18:15,889 --> 00:18:20,119
code that is separate from where
the agent code is actually running.

306
00:18:20,579 --> 00:18:25,969
So in AgentCore, AgentCore is AWS's
service for building agents, and it

307
00:18:25,979 --> 00:18:29,789
has multiple different components like
Runtime is a place where you can host

308
00:18:29,799 --> 00:18:34,559
agents, Gateway is that MCP server that
I was mentioning, or MCP proxy rather.

309
00:18:35,729 --> 00:18:38,929
And then there's another one called
Code Interpreter, and so this is an

310
00:18:39,019 --> 00:18:44,689
isolated runtime, an icu- an isolated
execution sandbox so that you can run

311
00:18:44,689 --> 00:18:49,199
code and your agents can run code in a
way that it doesn't have access to the

312
00:18:49,199 --> 00:18:52,599
internet, it doesn't have access to your
files or anything else in your system.

313
00:18:53,199 --> 00:18:57,429
In Strands also, there's something
called Strands Shell that allows you

314
00:18:57,429 --> 00:19:02,110
to give a shell to your agents, but you
can define specifically what network

315
00:19:02,110 --> 00:19:06,579
addresses that shell can access, what
files it can access, the types of

316
00:19:06,589 --> 00:19:08,739
actions that your agent can or cannot do.

317
00:19:09,170 --> 00:19:11,050
And that is all open source.

318
00:19:11,329 --> 00:19:13,350
So the Strands Shell is open source.

319
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,139
If you looked it up, you could go
dig into that and see how it works.

320
00:19:16,159 --> 00:19:19,479
I would really highly recommend
that you use that if you are giving

321
00:19:19,479 --> 00:19:23,711
shell access to your agents All
right, another scenario here.

322
00:19:24,261 --> 00:19:27,921
I don't trust my agent with
high-stakes transactions.

323
00:19:28,381 --> 00:19:31,811
In this case here, the person is
saying that they want a refund for

324
00:19:32,141 --> 00:19:35,371
eight hundred and ninety-nine dollars,
and our agent just issues the refund.

325
00:19:35,481 --> 00:19:39,081
But the problem is that the company
policy is that all refunds over five

326
00:19:39,081 --> 00:19:40,671
hundred dollars need human approval.

327
00:19:40,981 --> 00:19:46,671
So even though our agent had this in its
prompt, it still refunded the customer.

328
00:19:47,061 --> 00:19:52,311
So we need a way to block this sort
of thing in a deterministic fashion,

329
00:19:52,341 --> 00:19:53,971
not leave it up to the model itself.

330
00:19:55,181 --> 00:19:59,601
So what you need to do is have a
deterministic tool authorization layer.

331
00:19:59,941 --> 00:20:04,811
In AWS, there's something called
Agent Core Policy that allows

332
00:20:04,811 --> 00:20:07,051
you to write Dogwood policies.

333
00:20:07,401 --> 00:20:12,001
And Dogwood policies allow you to
evaluate who's asking, what are they

334
00:20:12,001 --> 00:20:13,541
doing, and should this be allowed.

335
00:20:13,821 --> 00:20:16,971
So which agent is asking
to call this tool?

336
00:20:17,401 --> 00:20:20,171
Under what circumstances are
they asking to call this tool?

337
00:20:20,171 --> 00:20:21,801
And is this allowed or denied?

338
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:26,561
Dogwood is also an open source
policy language released by AWS,

339
00:20:26,931 --> 00:20:31,391
and Agent Core Policy is a policy
engine that enforces those rules.

340
00:20:31,401 --> 00:20:34,392
So there's two separate things, the
policy and then the policy engine.

341
00:20:34,821 --> 00:20:39,591
So here we have an agent, a strands agent,
running an Agent Core Runtime, or a…

342
00:20:39,721 --> 00:20:41,291
It could also be any type of agent.

343
00:20:41,341 --> 00:20:43,462
Agent Core Runtime supports any framework.

344
00:20:43,621 --> 00:20:46,291
But we have an agent running an Agent
Core Runtime, makes a tool call.

345
00:20:46,612 --> 00:20:50,212
That tool call first goes through
the policy engine, and that

346
00:20:50,212 --> 00:20:51,971
Dogwood policy is enforced.

347
00:20:52,191 --> 00:20:56,072
If it is allowed, then it gets
sent to Agent Core Gateway.

348
00:20:56,422 --> 00:20:59,461
And you could also have that
MCP interceptor here as well, so

349
00:20:59,462 --> 00:21:01,571
you're seeing how you can start
to layer these things together.

350
00:21:01,882 --> 00:21:04,391
And then it invokes the tool, and
then flows all the way back through.

351
00:21:04,591 --> 00:21:09,942
So this allows you to have a centrally
governed place for tool authorization.

352
00:21:11,542 --> 00:21:14,422
This is what a Dogwood
policy language looks like.

353
00:21:14,662 --> 00:21:20,542
Dogwood is a superset of Cedar, which
is another open source policy language.

354
00:21:20,982 --> 00:21:25,261
So it allows you to do extra things
like temporal checks as well.

355
00:21:25,302 --> 00:21:30,532
But here we have if the principal is
an authenticated user, then I'm going

356
00:21:30,552 --> 00:21:34,182
to allow them to call the refund tool.

357
00:21:34,692 --> 00:21:35,212
Let's see.

358
00:21:35,222 --> 00:21:40,071
Yeah, I want to permit them to call
the refund tool when the principal has

359
00:21:40,102 --> 00:21:41,862
an amount under five hundred dollars.

360
00:21:42,152 --> 00:21:45,752
So this would deny any tool
call over five hundred dollars.

361
00:21:45,762 --> 00:21:48,872
So you can put it in your system
prompt, but that's not a guardrail.

362
00:21:48,932 --> 00:21:50,522
This is a deterministic guardrail.

363
00:21:50,652 --> 00:21:55,544
No agent is gonna be able to think
their way around this All right,

364
00:21:55,544 --> 00:21:58,304
and finally, you need to be able to
see what your agent is doing, right?

365
00:21:58,344 --> 00:22:02,394
Agents are able to take many
different actions in very quick

366
00:22:02,424 --> 00:22:06,184
succession, and observability is more
important than ever because of the

367
00:22:06,184 --> 00:22:07,854
agent's non-deterministic nature.

368
00:22:08,054 --> 00:22:12,624
So you wanna make sure you have auto
instrumenting… or sorry, auto tracing

369
00:22:12,644 --> 00:22:16,554
instrumented throughout your application,
and Strands does allow you to do this,

370
00:22:16,554 --> 00:22:18,304
and most major frameworks do as well.

371
00:22:18,304 --> 00:22:21,834
You just have to turn it on or collect
those metrics that they give you.

372
00:22:22,494 --> 00:22:27,554
This will help you pinpoint issues and
also track to see what your agent did.

373
00:22:27,554 --> 00:22:30,734
So if anything goes wrong, you can
go dig in there and take a look.

374
00:22:31,054 --> 00:22:34,854
But you also wanna set up anomaly
detection so that way if your tool

375
00:22:34,854 --> 00:22:38,474
call rate starts spiking like crazy,
or if your token consumption starts

376
00:22:38,484 --> 00:22:42,214
spiking like crazy, you can alert on
those anomalies and have someone go

377
00:22:42,214 --> 00:22:44,104
investigate before it gets out of control.

378
00:22:45,896 --> 00:22:49,676
All right, so this is the full
AWS architecture that I set up and

379
00:22:49,686 --> 00:22:55,946
deployed to my AWS account, and this
is where I have the robot vacuum

380
00:22:56,016 --> 00:22:57,736
customer service agent deployed.

381
00:22:58,106 --> 00:23:01,286
You can see we have the actual
Strands agent itself is hosted

382
00:23:01,286 --> 00:23:02,136
using AgentCore Runtime.

383
00:23:02,136 --> 00:23:07,046
AgentCore Runtime is a serverless
managed runtime that does isolated

384
00:23:07,046 --> 00:23:11,576
sessions for you, and it has a couple
of quality of life improvements for

385
00:23:11,576 --> 00:23:13,206
hosting and running agents at scale.

386
00:23:13,736 --> 00:23:15,776
But notice that's not the front door.

387
00:23:15,816 --> 00:23:18,006
For the client… Let me
bring our mouse over here.

388
00:23:18,706 --> 00:23:22,476
My client, whenever it submits a request,
first what they have to do is authenticate

389
00:23:22,476 --> 00:23:24,556
with Cognito, which is an OAuth provider.

390
00:23:24,556 --> 00:23:26,936
So you could swap that out
for any OAuth provider.

391
00:23:27,326 --> 00:23:31,276
Then it sends a request through
Amazon web application firewall.

392
00:23:31,466 --> 00:23:36,176
So this allows you to do things like bot
detection, it can… You can restrict

393
00:23:36,176 --> 00:23:37,846
by IP, all those sorts of things.

394
00:23:38,206 --> 00:23:42,296
Then we have a proper front door, like API
gateway, where you can add rate limiting.

395
00:23:43,086 --> 00:23:47,556
These two things give you a proper
place where you can shed load, and

396
00:23:47,566 --> 00:23:51,576
that's really important for AI-driven
applications, because you're essentially

397
00:23:51,576 --> 00:23:56,406
exposing a model to the internet, and
people are going to come and try to

398
00:23:56,416 --> 00:23:58,166
use that model and abuse that model.

399
00:23:58,176 --> 00:24:03,046
So you have to have specific ways to shed
that load and shut people down if needed.

400
00:24:03,696 --> 00:24:07,406
After that, only if the person is
authenticated would it ever reach the

401
00:24:07,426 --> 00:24:09,086
agent running an AgentCore Runtime.

402
00:24:10,286 --> 00:24:13,586
Once the agent decides that it wants
to invoke the tools, it will send

403
00:24:13,586 --> 00:24:16,196
out that tool request through MCP.

404
00:24:16,206 --> 00:24:20,366
So it will send out the MCP request, and
our tools are living all the way over

405
00:24:20,366 --> 00:24:23,796
here on our Lambda functions, but first
it has to jump through multiple hoops.

406
00:24:24,296 --> 00:24:29,036
First it hits AgentCore Policy, and
this will do the actual enforcement

407
00:24:29,036 --> 00:24:30,206
of those Dogwood policies.

408
00:24:30,216 --> 00:24:31,126
So who's calling?

409
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:31,936
What are they trying to do?

410
00:24:31,946 --> 00:24:32,526
Is it allowed?

411
00:24:33,116 --> 00:24:35,746
And after that, it goes to
AgentCore Gateway, which is

412
00:24:35,756 --> 00:24:37,336
like our managed MCP server.

413
00:24:37,336 --> 00:24:42,036
It's a proxy for MCP, and this
will run the MCP interceptor.

414
00:24:42,336 --> 00:24:45,796
The MCP interceptor will do things
like custom validation logic.

415
00:24:45,816 --> 00:24:48,086
It will supply se-sensitive parameters.

416
00:24:48,346 --> 00:24:55,296
So in the use case before, where somebody
was providing a value that was sensitive,

417
00:24:55,316 --> 00:24:56,886
like can you look up my order ID?

418
00:24:57,206 --> 00:25:01,806
Instead of having the agent provide
the order ID, we have the authenticated

419
00:25:01,806 --> 00:25:06,446
token that comes through, and this
interceptor is able to extract that

420
00:25:06,446 --> 00:25:11,576
customer ID and validate that the order
actually belongs to that customer before

421
00:25:11,576 --> 00:25:13,576
passing it along to the Lambda function.

422
00:25:14,026 --> 00:25:16,806
And then the Lambda function
would actually execute the tools.

423
00:25:17,336 --> 00:25:19,486
There's a couple of other
things here on this diagram.

424
00:25:19,826 --> 00:25:23,576
Bedrock Guardrails provides that input
and output filtering, and then you

425
00:25:23,576 --> 00:25:29,066
would use IAM to do least privilege
permissions, both for the agents

426
00:25:29,076 --> 00:25:34,302
running and also the tools that are
running in the account as well So this

427
00:25:34,302 --> 00:25:36,782
shows you a layered approach to trust.

428
00:25:36,872 --> 00:25:39,702
And this just shows you the
different dimensions, the risk,

429
00:25:39,742 --> 00:25:43,142
the control layer, and whether it's
deterministic or probabilistic.

430
00:25:43,652 --> 00:25:47,592
So for your prompt injection,
you-- the risk is instruction

431
00:25:47,602 --> 00:25:49,022
hijacking, those sorts of things.

432
00:25:49,022 --> 00:25:52,522
You would wanna use Bedrock guardrails
for that, and this is probabilistic

433
00:25:52,552 --> 00:25:54,392
in nature, so it's not deterministic.

434
00:25:54,922 --> 00:26:00,052
For tools, this is going to be tool
authorization, agent steering, that

435
00:26:00,052 --> 00:26:04,552
policy layer, all of those things layer
on top of each other to make it safer

436
00:26:04,552 --> 00:26:09,952
for agents to call tools, as well as the
sh- the strands shell and sandbox tool

437
00:26:09,952 --> 00:26:11,652
execution and code execution as well.

438
00:26:12,472 --> 00:26:15,222
For your actual agent runtime,
you wanna make sure that you have

439
00:26:15,222 --> 00:26:17,382
sandbox agent runtime environments.

440
00:26:17,932 --> 00:26:22,732
For data, to protect against sensitive
data exposure, you can use guardrails for

441
00:26:22,732 --> 00:26:26,702
data filtering, tool design to make sure
you're not exposing tools and information

442
00:26:26,702 --> 00:26:31,532
to agents that they don't need, and
also steering policies can help you

443
00:26:31,602 --> 00:26:36,292
strip out or deny those model responses
that do have sensitive information.

444
00:26:37,272 --> 00:26:41,732
For network, you need to set up
egress and DNS controls for your agent

445
00:26:42,162 --> 00:26:46,042
because for through prompt injection,
somebody could tell your agent to go

